# Streams and `Optional` together

Not neighbours by accident — the **same idea at two scales.** A stream is a pipeline over *many*; an `Optional` over *zero or one*.

## They speak the same language

```java
stream.map(f).filter(p)      // over many
optional.map(f).filter(p)    // over zero-or-one
```

## The natural handoff: search that might find nothing

```java
String top = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> e.dept() == ENGINEERING)
    .max(comparingInt(Employee::salary))   // Optional<Employee>
    .map(Employee::name)                   // Optional<String>
    .orElse("no engineers");               // leave the box
```

Search → extract → default, as one sentence — empty handled by construction.

## The other direction: flatten Optionals back to a stream

```java
List<Email> emails = users.stream()
    .map(User::email)            // Stream<Optional<Email>> — awkward
    .flatMap(Optional::stream)   // empty → 0, present → 1
    .toList();                   // only the present ones
```

## The module in one line

Lambda → value · stream → pipeline · lazy → fuse & short-circuit · collect / reduce / parallel · **`Optional` = the zero-or-one case, absence as a *type*.** Describe the transform; get a value — empty honest at every step.
